%I A401636 #4 Nov 05 2026 21:02:15
%S A401636 1,2,8,18,72,128,196,200,288,392,968,1568,1764,1800,2178,3200,3528,
%T A401636 5000,5776,6272,7056,7200,7938,9522,9604,12544,12996,15876,16200,
%U A401636 16928,17298,19208,19600,19602,23716,24336,25088,25992,27378,28224
%N A401636 Practical numbers M (A005153) such that sigma(M) + 2 is prime.
%C A401636 A positive integer n is practical (A005153) if every integer 1 <= k <= sigma(n) can be expressed as a sum of distinct divisors of n.
%C A401636 For each term M, since q = sigma(M) + 2 is an odd prime, sigma(M) must be odd. Hence, in the prime factorization of M, the exponent of every odd prime factor must be even, so M is of the form 2^e * s^2 where s is an odd integer.
%C A401636 For each term M, the product B = M * q generates the root base of almost practical numbers with a single missing sum (A174533): every integer 1 <= k <= sigma(M) is representable as a sum of divisors of M, but H = sigma(M) + 1 cannot be formed by any subset sum of divisors of B, making H the first unrepresented value.
%e A401636 a(1) = 1: 1 is practical, sigma(1) + 2 = 1 + 2 = 3 is prime.
%e A401636 a(2) = 2: 2 is practical, sigma(2) + 2 = 3 + 2 = 5 is prime.
%e A401636 a(3) = 8: 8 is practical, sigma(8) + 2 = 15 + 2 = 17 is prime.
%e A401636 a(4) = 18: 18 is practical, sigma(18) + 2 = 39 + 2 = 41 is prime.
%o A401636 (Python)
%o A401636 from sympy import factorint, divisor_sigma, isprime
%o A401636 def is_practical(m):
%o A401636     if m == 1:
%o A401636         return True
%o A401636     factors = factorint(m)
%o A401636     if 2 not in factors:
%o A401636         return False
%o A401636     sig = 1
%o A401636     for p in sorted(factors):
%o A401636         if p > sig + 1:
%o A401636             return False
%o A401636         sig *= (p**(factors[p] + 1) - 1) // (p - 1)
%o A401636     return True
%o A401636 print([m for m in range(1, 30000) if is_practical(m) and isprime(divisor_sigma(m) + 2)])
%Y A401636 Cf. A000040, A000203, A005153, A174533, A174534.
%K A401636 nonn,new
%O A401636 1,2
